    <section>
      <h2>1. The Big One: This Is Not Medical Advice</h2>
      <p>We cannot stress this enough: <strong>Nothing on Calorie Calculator Free is a substitute for professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment.</strong></p>
      <p>To give you a concrete example: Let's say you're dealing with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), a thyroid condition, or you are recovering from an eating disorder. Our calculators run on standard mathematical formulas like the Mifflin-St Jeor or Harris-Benedict equations. These equations are brilliant statistical models based on population averages, but they are just that—averages. They have never met you. They don't know your medical history, your hormonal fluctuations, or your unique metabolic adaptations.</p>
      <p>When our tool tells you that your Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) is 2,200 calories, that is a <em>starting estimate</em>, not a prescription from a doctor. If you are pregnant, nursing, managing diabetes, or dealing with any health condition, you must speak with a physician or a Registered Dietitian before making significant changes to your diet. By using our service, you acknowledge that you are taking responsibility for your own body and your own choices. We highly recommend reading our full <a href="/disclaimer/">Medical Disclaimer</a> for a deeper dive into this topic.</p>
    </section>

