    <section>
      <h2>4. Accuracy of Information and Nutritional Data</h2>
      <p>We pride ourselves on accuracy, but the world of nutrition is notoriously complex. Let's look at a real-world example: A"medium apple." If you select a medium apple in a food tracker, you might see it listed as 95 calories. But what if your apple spent more time on the tree? What if it's slightly larger than the agricultural standard? It might actually be 110 calories.</p>
      <p>The nutritional data provided on this site, including macronutrient breakdowns and caloric estimates, are drawn from highly reputable sources like the USDA. However, they are still estimates. Food labels in the United States are legally allowed a 20% margin of error. Therefore, we cannot guarantee the absolute precision of every single calorie or gram of protein listed on this site. You agree that you are using this data with the understanding that slight variations are normal and expected. We are not liable if a specific food item's nutritional profile changes or varies from our database.</p>
    </section>

